---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro';
import Card from '@ui/Card.vue';

const docs = (await getCollection('docs')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order || a.id.localeCompare(b.id));
const skills = (await getCollection('skills')).sort((a, b) => a.id.localeCompare(b.id));
---

<Layout title="Documentation">
  <h1>Voxie UI Documentation</h1>

  <Card surface='layered' padding='spacious'>

  <p class="mt-0">The design system behind Voxie. The components screens are built from, the foundations they sit on, and the guidance for putting the two together.</p>

  <p class="mb-0">It is written to be read twice: once by a person deciding how a screen should look, and once by an agent building it. The docs carry the reasoning, and the skills restate the same rules as instructions an agent can follow.</p>

  </Card>

  <h2>What's here</h2>

  <h3 class="mt-0 mb-0">Docs</h3>
  <p class="">Foundations, components, patterns and guidance, with the reasoning left in rather than boiled down to rules.</p>

  <h3 class="mt-0 mb-0">Skills</h3>
  <p class="">The same ground written as instructions, so generated UI lands inside the system instead of beside it.</p>

  <h3 class="mt-0 mb-0">Test&nbsp;pages</h3>
  <p class="">Nine real Voxie screens stripped of every class, for trying a direction end to end before committing to it.</p>

  <h2>Where to start</h2>

  <p>
    <a href="/docs/foundations/color">Color</a> and{' '}
    <a href="/docs/foundations/typography">Typography</a> are the furthest along, and everything else assumes them. Much of the rest is still an outline waiting to be filled in.
  </p>
</Layout>
